WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Bug bounty is easy, is what we think
when we first hear about it and

00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:10.769
we see thousands of dollars being
awarded for not so complex bugs.

00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:12.549
But is it really?

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:18.429
I'm not a person that started their
cybersecurity career 20 years ago, and

00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:20.579
I didn't hack since primary school.

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:24.609
I was a regular dude who studied
computer science, started working as

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:30.149
a pentester, and two years ago I quit
to do bug bounty and create content.

00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:33.779
I'm sharing with you
details of my journey.

00:00:33.959 --> 00:00:38.499
And in this video, I will tell you
about my second year of bug bounty with

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:41.709
precise number of reports and earnings.

00:00:41.999 --> 00:00:43.836
Enjoy!  

00:00:46.048 --> 00:00:51.278
One day I logged into my
WordPress website and I saw an alert(1).

00:00:51.278 --> 00:00:56.358
At first, I thought it's some kind of
joke by a plugin developer, but turned

00:00:56.358 --> 00:01:02.058
out that one of you tried an XSS payload
in the search field on my website.

00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:07.702
And it didn't work for you but it
did trigger the XSS on the backend.

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:14.999
It was a zero day in MonsterInsights
plugin used by over 3 million

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:18.689
websites, which makes it one
of the most popular WordPress

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:19.409
plugins.

00:01:19.938 --> 00:01:23.718
A bug like this is great because
not only you can report it to the

00:01:23.728 --> 00:01:29.678
developers of the plugin, but also
to every single website that uses the

00:01:29.698 --> 00:01:31.498
plugin and has a bug bounty program.

00:01:31.748 --> 00:01:34.848
So you can get multiple
bounties for one bug.

00:01:35.248 --> 00:01:40.578
And since this plugin was so popular,
I knew it's worth thousands of dollars.

00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:46.018
I sort of wanted to to find out
who actually found it and let you

00:01:46.048 --> 00:01:47.478
know that you just found the bug.

00:01:47.778 --> 00:01:52.678
But it was just impossible to track who
initiated this search on my website.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.926
So I decided to do it myself and I
collaborated with my friend Dawid,

00:01:58.176 --> 00:02:02.106
who I knew does a lot of bug bounty
automations, which by the way are now

00:02:02.146 --> 00:02:04.136
available as a product called Vidoc.

00:02:04.956 --> 00:02:09.936
In total, we found 19 websites with
bug bounty programs using this plugin.

00:02:10.446 --> 00:02:11.196
And we made.

00:02:13.196 --> 00:02:14.966
We made $0 from it.

00:02:15.256 --> 00:02:21.256
The problem was that it was quite a
complex blind XSS because  It triggered

00:02:21.286 --> 00:02:26.451
in a very specific functionality and also
depended on the analytics backend used.

00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:32.021
We just didn't get blind XSS pingbacks
to XSSHunter, and to be honest, I'm

00:02:32.021 --> 00:02:36.721
still not exactly sure why, but in
hindsight, I should have spent more

00:02:36.721 --> 00:02:42.011
time using this plugin with different
analytics backends to create a payload

00:02:42.011 --> 00:02:44.852
suitable for every single integration.

00:02:45.351 --> 00:02:50.301
One way or another, it was a huge
disappointment because in theory,

00:02:50.311 --> 00:02:55.791
this bug affected 3 million websites
and I couldn't even make $1 out of it.

00:02:56.361 --> 00:03:01.292
But it definitely made me hungry for
more scalable zero days like this.

00:03:01.951 --> 00:03:06.301
Later, I was hacking on Discourse,
an open source forum, and among

00:03:06.361 --> 00:03:11.691
other bugs, I found an XSS which I
knew I can report to many programs.

00:03:12.414 --> 00:03:18.044
First, myself, I found one vulnerable
instance, and I got $2,000 from it.

00:03:19.444 --> 00:03:24.944
It made me really happy and quite proud
because it was the first case of a

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:31.164
bounty for a scalable zero day and it
was a decent reward of $2,000 as well.

00:03:32.797 --> 00:03:34.867
I also reached out to Nagli.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:39.872
He's one of the best hackers out
there and he relies on automation.

00:03:39.877 --> 00:03:41.647
He found two more instances.

00:03:41.657 --> 00:03:43.257
So we filed two reports.

00:03:43.707 --> 00:03:47.327
One of them is in the new
state since 10 months.

00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:53.147
And the other one is quite interesting
because first, Bugcrowd triaged it.

00:03:53.672 --> 00:03:56.532
But then it was closed
because of no CSP bypass.

00:03:57.282 --> 00:04:02.292
We were confused with Nagli because we
only reported it to programs with no CSP

00:04:02.302 --> 00:04:05.042
in place since we didn't have a bypass.

00:04:05.922 --> 00:04:10.352
Turned out that they had planned to
enable CSP anyway, even before they

00:04:10.352 --> 00:04:16.742
got our report and they implemented
it after Bugcrowd triaging our report

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:19.342
and before security team doing it.

00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:23.322
But we opened a support ticket on
Bugcrowd  and they were quick to

00:04:23.352 --> 00:04:25.192
respond and resolve the situation.

00:04:25.522 --> 00:04:29.202
And we got $3,600 of
bounty for this report.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:35.450
So shout out to the Bugcrowd team
for doing a great job here and to the

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.730
program as well, because they resolved
the situation, which was actually

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.590
just a unfortunate timing situation.

00:04:43.263 --> 00:04:50.213
in total, I got $512 from the vendor,
$2,000 from one instance, and then

00:04:50.253 --> 00:04:54.043
$3,600 split in half for another one.

00:04:54.771 --> 00:04:58.071
And when collaborating, even
though technically you only get

00:04:58.071 --> 00:05:02.171
50% of the bounty, you
are not actually losing anything.

00:05:02.491 --> 00:05:07.471
Because if you find a zero day, what you
should do is first, you should try to

00:05:07.471 --> 00:05:13.341
find all vulnerable instances yourself
and report it to them by yourself.

00:05:13.451 --> 00:05:17.671
And only then, after you have no
more ideas on how to detect more,

00:05:18.071 --> 00:05:22.341
Then you go to a person like Nagli
who does the automations and you

00:05:22.411 --> 00:05:24.331
ask them to find more instances.

00:05:24.651 --> 00:05:28.891
You are not losing anything because
you wouldn't find those instances

00:05:28.931 --> 00:05:34.391
anyway, and the other person, of course,
also benefits, so everyone is happy.

00:05:34.531 --> 00:05:38.881
So don't hesitate to contact
someone that does automations

00:05:38.941 --> 00:05:41.231
when you find a scalable zero day.

00:05:42.192 --> 00:05:44.322
For me, it was one of my goals.

00:05:44.372 --> 00:05:47.932
And after achieving it, I decided
to switch things up a bit.

00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:51.752
I invested more time into learning CodeQL.

00:05:51.962 --> 00:05:55.962
It's not a regular bug bounty program,
but I will not go into details here

00:05:56.182 --> 00:06:00.732
because I covered it in another video
and in multiple articles in BBRE Premium.

00:06:01.147 --> 00:06:05.527
My investment paid out really well
because I got my highest bounty to

00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:10.677
date of $5,500 for a CodeQL query to detect

00:06:10.817 --> 00:06:13.157
arbitrary file write by ZipSlip.

00:06:14.027 --> 00:06:18.087
And you may think that everything
just goes according to plan

00:06:18.117 --> 00:06:19.957
for me, but it's not true.

00:06:20.688 --> 00:06:24.328
Later, I also found an
authentication bypass in Mastodon.

00:06:24.598 --> 00:06:28.358
They don't have a bug bounty program,
but I did it for the purpose of

00:06:28.358 --> 00:06:30.278
writing another CodeQL query.

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:34.148
But because of some strange
things happening with Ruby,

00:06:34.238 --> 00:06:36.158
I just couldn't make it work.

00:06:36.508 --> 00:06:41.438
I also hacked on many different
regular bug bounty programs, but I

00:06:41.808 --> 00:06:44.658
couldn't stick to one for long enough.

00:06:45.192 --> 00:06:48.752
I also found an XSS in one
of the Google's products.

00:06:49.122 --> 00:06:53.042
I got a 'Nice catch!' and the report
was triaged for a few weeks.

00:06:54.227 --> 00:06:59.917
However, when deciding the reward,
they decided to reward me with $0.

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:06.022
It was actually a decision I could
understand, it was just quite painful

00:07:06.052 --> 00:07:10.312
that this decision was made so
late when I was already expecting a

00:07:10.312 --> 00:07:12.612
bounty of $3k or something like this.

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:17.339
Then there was another zero
day that I wanted to scale.

00:07:17.709 --> 00:07:23.199
I stumbled upon the NodeBB forum
when doing one of the case studies I

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:26.899
found an XSS quite quickly, reported
it to the vendor and I started

00:07:26.899 --> 00:07:28.669
looking for vulnerable instances.

00:07:29.344 --> 00:07:34.614
I found one, and then I also collaborated
with Michael Ness to detect more.

00:07:35.594 --> 00:07:40.230
one program closed it as informational,
the other one resolved it,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.120
but without paying any bounty.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.040
There was however one Bugcrowd program,
which I was really hopeful for.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:50.070
They were active and
triaged the bug quickly.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:55.630
We collaborated and I gave them the
monkey patch to fix the vulnerability

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.390
before the official fix is released.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.660
I thought I will get a $1,500

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:02.430
payout from them.

00:08:03.914 --> 00:08:08.334
At this point, it was a few months
after I came back to Poland after

00:08:08.334 --> 00:08:12.764
winter in Tenerife, and I just couldn't
turn on the hacking mode again.

00:08:13.134 --> 00:08:18.444
At this point, I would welcome any 4-digit bounty to break this dry spell.

00:08:18.875 --> 00:08:24.025
but they only paid me $100, saying
they usually don't pay for zero

00:08:24.025 --> 00:08:28.425
days at all, but they wanted to
reward me for good collaboration.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:33.080
So, while it was a nice compliment
to hear, you know, I would

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.857
rather hear curse words with $1,500 bounty

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:45.410
So it turned out that the $256
reward from the vendor was my

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.540
highest bounty for this zero day.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:50.390
So again, quite a lot
of disappointment here.

00:08:51.641 --> 00:08:56.861
And in the bounty vlog one year ago,
I complained how little time I spent

00:08:56.861 --> 00:09:00.111
on bug bounty and I planned to change.

00:09:00.731 --> 00:09:01.491
And I did.

00:09:02.331 --> 00:09:08.371
But not for the better, I spent little
time overall doing hacking, but even

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:11.431
worse, I jumped between programs a lot.

00:09:11.849 --> 00:09:16.519
I didn't have the persistence, the
persistence, which I think is the number

00:09:16.519 --> 00:09:22.229
one thing that every single experienced
hunter mentions in every single interview.

00:09:22.805 --> 00:09:26.985
Like take a look at how many different
programs I've tried this year,

00:09:26.985 --> 00:09:29.925
and the most time I spent on one.

00:09:30.385 --> 00:09:36.045
is 40 hours, with a total of
barely over 200 hours of hunting.

00:09:38.045 --> 00:09:43.375
So, in some sense, I'm closing in
on Tim Ferriss 4 hour workweek.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.490
But jokes aside,

00:09:45.986 --> 00:09:48.926
Remember, did I log my time
in a very specific way

00:09:48.926 --> 00:09:53.276
and it doesn't make sense for you
to compare yourself to, to my time.

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:57.566
But it makes a lot of sense for me
to compare myself year over year.

00:09:58.046 --> 00:10:01.136
And it is much less time
than in the previous year.

00:10:01.806 --> 00:10:05.776
it's not easy making a video like
this, basically admitting to a lot

00:10:05.776 --> 00:10:08.096
of procrastination and time wasting.

00:10:08.196 --> 00:10:11.826
In fact, my friend, Dawid, whom I
mentioned before, had to sort of

00:10:11.826 --> 00:10:15.946
convince me to do it because I was
like, "maybe, maybe I shouldn't".

00:10:16.686 --> 00:10:22.076
But he reminded me that the goal of this
series is to give you the real image,

00:10:22.116 --> 00:10:26.856
to tell you how it actually looks like,
even if it's not a quick success story,

00:10:27.468 --> 00:10:30.798
My perspective also changed
a lot since last year.

00:10:31.288 --> 00:10:34.978
Then, I felt quite insecure
about my income and I was very

00:10:34.978 --> 00:10:37.138
disappointed with my time management.

00:10:37.585 --> 00:10:42.065
But this year, I felt much more
stability and I allowed myself

00:10:42.155 --> 00:10:46.165
to design the lifestyle that
I want and simply said, enjoy.

00:10:46.675 --> 00:10:52.965
I travelled a lot. I spent over 100 days
in Spain, Portugal and others, met dozens

00:10:52.965 --> 00:10:55.065
of great people and had lots of fun.

00:10:55.525 --> 00:11:00.845
And in the moments of bitching myself for
not working more, I have to remind myself

00:11:00.955 --> 00:11:03.825
that that's what I quit my 9 to 5 for.

00:11:04.402 --> 00:11:08.662
Another complaint that I had to
myself was that I spent too much

00:11:08.702 --> 00:11:12.842
time on the business side of
things and not enough time hunting.

00:11:13.542 --> 00:11:18.432
This also has changed a lot because
since I started doing case studies in

00:11:18.432 --> 00:11:23.692
BBRE Premium where I extract all the
reports about the particular bug class,

00:11:23.712 --> 00:11:28.782
and I do the analysis. Since I've started
doing those, BBRE Premium really caught

00:11:28.787 --> 00:11:33.732
traction and is now at the moment where
I could only live off that income.

00:11:34.732 --> 00:11:37.102
It takes a lot of
pressure off my shoulders.

00:11:37.677 --> 00:11:41.767
And I should probably also outsource
more to other people because I do

00:11:41.807 --> 00:11:47.427
too much myself, but outsourcing is a
difficult skill, which I'm only learning.

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:49.199
And you know what?

00:11:49.469 --> 00:11:56.249
Bug bounty may seem random at times, but
it does reward good work in the long run.

00:11:56.939 --> 00:12:03.449
When I started hunting and I made $7,000
in 100 hours on the Stripe challenge, I

00:12:03.449 --> 00:12:09.329
thought to myself, if I can make $7,000
out of a bunch of mediums, what will

00:12:09.329 --> 00:12:12.689
happen when I find a $10,000 or a $15,000 critical?

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:19.810
And it did eventually happen, apart it
wasn't a $10,000 or $15,000 bug and it happened

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:23.440
in such a low time bounty-wise for me.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:30.720
I got $20,000 for a path traversal on a
private program on Bugcrowd and it wiped

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:35.740
off completely any remaining pressure to
find bugs and also showed me that there

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.370
is no magic in those 5-digit payouts.

00:12:38.846 --> 00:12:42.856
So in total, I more than doubled
the hourly rate in the second

00:12:42.926 --> 00:12:44.376
year compared to the first year.

00:12:44.666 --> 00:12:50.416
And I even increased the total income,
even though I had way fewer findings.

00:12:51.696 --> 00:12:55.136
So can you live off bug bounties?

00:12:55.136 --> 00:13:01.356
For me, there is absolutely no doubt that
if I shut down YouTube and BBRE premium

00:13:01.376 --> 00:13:06.586
today, and I started focusing only on
bug bounty, I would make a great living.

00:13:07.938 --> 00:13:09.588
The plan for the next year?

00:13:09.973 --> 00:13:13.043
I'd really love to go
to a live hacking event.

00:13:13.113 --> 00:13:18.253
In fact, I think it would work quite well
because I see that I'm just a horrible

00:13:18.313 --> 00:13:23.823
multitasker and having to focus on a
live hacking event target for a few weeks

00:13:24.343 --> 00:13:30.123
to then focus only on creating content
for a few weeks could work just well.

00:13:30.468 --> 00:13:35.768
And also, I should outsource more to have
more time for hacking and I should stop

00:13:35.768 --> 00:13:38.608
bitching myself for not working so much.

00:13:38.748 --> 00:13:43.788
I want to keep traveling and digital
nomading and basically embrace my

00:13:43.788 --> 00:13:48.648
twenties because sometimes I feel
like I'm too serious for my age.

00:13:48.928 --> 00:13:54.758
I also urge you to design a lifestyle
that you want and not only blindly

00:13:54.758 --> 00:13:57.028
follow the masses or social pressure.

00:13:57.418 --> 00:14:01.528
I can tell you from my experience that
either running a business or doing

00:14:01.538 --> 00:14:06.478
bug bounty with unreliable income
is not as scary as you might think.

